= AsciiDoc Processing and Built-in Converters <asciidoc-processing-and-built-in-converters>

AsciiDoc is the language.\
Asciidoctor is the processor.

Asciidoctor reads the AsciiDoc source, as shown in the panel on the left in the image below, and converts it to publishable formats, such as HTML 5, as shown rendered in the panel on the right.

#link("https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/asciidoctor/asciidoctor/screenshot.png")[Preview of AsciiDoc source and corresponding rendered HTML]

Asciidoctor provides built-in #link("https://docs.asciidoctor.org/asciidoctor/latest/converters/")[converters] for three output formats by default: #link("https://docs.asciidoctor.org/asciidoctor/latest/html-backend/")[HTML 5], #link("https://docs.asciidoctor.org/asciidoctor/latest/docbook-backend/")[DocBook 5], and #link("https://docs.asciidoctor.org/asciidoctor/latest/manpage-backend/")[man page] (short for manual page).
Additional converters, such as PDF and EPUB 3, are provided by separate gems.
Asciidoctor also provides an out-of-the-box HTML experience complete with a #link("https://docs.asciidoctor.org/asciidoctor/latest/html-backend/default-stylesheet/")[default stylesheet] and built-in integrations like Font Awesome (for icons), highlight.js, Rouge, and Pygments (for source highlighting), and MathJax (for STEM processing).

= Asciidoctor Ecosystem <asciidoctor-ecosystem>

Although Asciidoctor is written in Ruby, it does not mean you need Ruby to use it.
Asciidoctor can be executed on a JVM using #link("https://docs.asciidoctor.org/asciidoctorj/latest/")[AsciidoctorJ] or in any JavaScript environment (including the browser) using #link("https://docs.asciidoctor.org/asciidoctor.js/latest/")[Asciidoctor.js].

Installing an Asciidoctor processor is just the beginning of your publishing experience.
Asciidoctor gives you access to a ecosystem of extensions and tools, ranging from add-on converters, to extended syntax, to build plugins, to integrated writing and preview environments:

- #link("https://docs.asciidoctor.org/diagram-extension/latest/")[Asciidoctor Diagram]
- #link("https://docs.asciidoctor.org/maven-tools/latest/")[Maven plugin and site module]
- #link("https://github.com/asciidoctor/asciidoctor-gradle-plugin")[Gradle plugin]
- #link("https://docs.asciidoctor.org/asciidoclet/latest/")[Asciidoclet]
- #link("https://docs.asciidoctor.org/reveal.js-converter/latest/")[reveal.js converter]
- #link("https://docs.asciidoctor.org/epub3-converter/latest/")[EPUB 3 converter]
- #link("https://intellij-asciidoc-plugin.ahus1.de/docs")[IntelliJ plugin]
- #link("https://docs.asciidoctor.org/asciidoctor/latest/tooling/#web-browser-add-ons-preview-only")[web browser extensions]
- #link("https://github.com/asciidoctor")[and more]

Asciidoctor is the successor to AsciiDoc.py.
If you’re using AsciiDoc.py, see #link("https://docs.asciidoctor.org/asciidoctor/latest/migrate/asciidoc-py/")[Migrate from AsciiDoc.py] to learn how to upgrade to Asciidoctor.

= Requirements <requirements>

Asciidoctor works on Linux, macOS and Windows and requires one of the following implementations of #link("https://www.ruby-lang.org")[Ruby]:

- CRuby (aka MRI) 2.7 - 3.4
- JRuby 9.4.0 - 10.0.2
- TruffleRuby (GraalVM)

#adoc-admonition("caution")[
If you’re using a non-English Windows environment, you may bump into an #raw("Encoding::UndefinedConversionError") when invoking Asciidoctor.
To solve this issue, we recommend overriding the default external and internal character encodings to #raw("utf-8").
You can do so by setting the #raw("RUBYOPT") environment variable as follows:

#raw(block: true, "RUBYOPT=\"-E utf-8:utf-8\"")

Once you make this change, all your Unicode headaches should be behind you.

If you’re using an IDE like Eclipse, make sure you set the encoding to UTF-8 there as well.
Asciidoctor is optimized to work with UTF-8 as the default encoding.
]

= Installation <installation>

Asciidoctor is packaged and distributed to RubyGems.org as a RubyGem (aka gem) named #link("https://rubygems.org/gems/asciidoctor")[asciidoctor].
The asciidoctor gem can be installed on all major operating systems using Ruby packaging tools (gem or bundle).
Asciidoctor is also distributed as a Docker image, as a package for numerous Linux distributions, and as a package for macOS (via Homebrew and MacPorts).

== Linux package managers <linux-package-managers>

The version of Asciidoctor installed by the package manager may not match the latest release of Asciidoctor.
Consult the package repository for your distribution to find out which version is packaged per distribution release.

- #link("https://pkgs.alpinelinux.org/packages?name=asciidoctor")[Alpine Linux (asciidoctor)]
- #link("https://www.archlinux.org/packages/?name=asciidoctor")[Arch Linux (asciidoctor)]
- #link("https://packages.debian.org/sid/asciidoctor")[Debian (asciidoctor)]
- #link("https://apps.fedoraproject.org/packages/rubygem-asciidoctor")[Fedora (asciidoctor)]
- #link("https://software.opensuse.org/package/rubygem-asciidoctor")[OpenSUSE (rubygem-asciidoctor)]
- #link("https://packages.ubuntu.com/search?keywords=asciidoctor")[Ubuntu (asciidoctor)]

If you want to use a version of Asciidoctor that’s newer than what is installed by the package manager, see the #link(<gem-install>)[gem installation instructions].

=== apk (Alpine Linux) <apk-alpine-linux>

To install the gem on Alpine Linux, open a terminal and type:

#raw(block: true, "$ sudo apk add asciidoctor")

=== pacman (Arch Linux) <pacman-arch-linux>

To install the gem on Arch-based distributions, open a terminal and type:

#raw(block: true, "$ sudo pacman -S asciidoctor")

=== APT <apt>

On Debian and Debian-based distributions such as Ubuntu, use APT to install Asciidoctor.
To install the package, open a terminal and type:

#raw(block: true, "$ sudo apt-get install -y asciidoctor")

=== DNF <dnf>

On RPM-based Linux distributions, such as Fedora, CentOS, and RHEL, use the DNF package manager to install Asciidoctor.
To install the package, open a terminal and type:

#raw(block: true, "$ sudo dnf install -y asciidoctor")

== macOS <macos>

=== Homebrew <homebrew>

You can use #link("https://brew.sh")[Homebrew], the macOS package manager, to install Asciidoctor.
If you don’t have Homebrew on your computer, complete the #link("https://brew.sh")[installation instructions] first.

Once Homebrew is installed, you’re ready to install the #raw("asciidoctor") gem.
Open a terminal and type:

#raw(block: true, "$ brew install asciidoctor")

Homebrew installs the #raw("asciidoctor") gem into an exclusive prefix that’s independent of system gems.

=== MacPorts <macports>

You can also use #link("https://www.macports.org")[MacPorts], another package manager for macOS, to install Asciidoctor.
If you don’t have MacPorts on your computer, complete the #link("https://www.macports.org/install.php")[installation instructions] first.

Once MacPorts is installed, you’re ready to install the #raw("asciidoctor") gem via the #link("https://ports.macports.org/port/asciidoctor/")[Asciidoctor port].
Open a terminal and type:

#raw(block: true, "$ sudo port install asciidoctor")

== Windows <windows>

To use Asciidoctor with Windows, you have two options.

=== Chocolatey <chocolatey>

When you already use #link("https://chocolatey.org")[chocolatey] on your machine, you can use:

#raw(block: true, "choco install ruby")

Then follow #link(<gem-install>)[gem installation instructions].

=== Rubyinstaller <rubyinstaller>

Or you use the #link("https://rubyinstaller.org/downloads/")[Rubyinstaller], download the package for your Windows Version and after the installation go ahead with #link(<gem-install>)[gem installation instructions].

== gem install <gem-install>

Before installing Asciidoctor using #raw("gem install"), you should set up #link("https://rvm.io")[RVM] (or similar) to install Ruby in your home directory (i.e., user space).
Then, you can safely use the #raw("gem") command to install or update the Asciidoctor gem, or any other gem for that matter.
When using RVM, gems are installed in a location isolated from the system.
(You should never use the gem command to install system-wide gems).

Once you’ve installed Ruby using RVM, and you have activated it using #raw("rvm use 3.0"), open a terminal and type:

#raw(block: true, "$ gem install asciidoctor")

If you want to install a pre-release version (e.g., a release candidate), use:

#raw(block: true, "$ gem install asciidoctor --pre")

== Docker <docker>

See #link("https://github.com/asciidoctor/docker-asciidoctor")[Installing Asciidoctor using Docker].

== Bundler <bundler>

+ Create a Gemfile in the root folder of your project (or the current directory)
+ Add the #raw("asciidoctor") gem to your Gemfile as follows:

  #raw(block: true, lang: "ruby", "source 'https://rubygems.org'\ngem 'asciidoctor'\n# or specify the version explicitly\n# gem 'asciidoctor', '2.0.22'")
+ Save the Gemfile
+ Open a terminal and install the gem using:

  #raw(block: true, "$ bundle")

To upgrade the gem, specify the new version in the Gemfile and run #raw("bundle") again.
Using #raw("bundle update") (without specifying a gem) is #strong[not] recommended as it will also update other gems, which may not be the desired result.

= Upgrade <upgrade>

If you installed Asciidoctor using a package manager, your operating system is probably configured to automatically update packages, in which case you don’t need to update the gem manually.

== apk (Alpine Linux) <apk-alpine-linux-2>

To upgrade the gem, use:

#raw(block: true, "$ sudo apk add -u asciidoctor")

== APT <apt-2>

To upgrade the gem, use:

#raw(block: true, "$ sudo apt-get upgrade -y asciidoctor")

== DNF <dnf-2>

To upgrade the gem, use:

#raw(block: true, "$ sudo dnf update -y asciidoctor")

== Homebrew (macOS) <homebrew-macos>

To upgrade the gem, use:

#raw(block: true, "$ brew update\n$ brew upgrade asciidoctor")

== MacPorts (macOS) <macports-macos>

To upgrade the gem, use:

#raw(block: true, "$ sudo port selfupdate\n$ sudo port upgrade asciidoctor")

== gem install <gem-install-2>

If you previously installed Asciidoctor using the #raw("gem") command, you’ll need to manually upgrade Asciidoctor when a new version is released.
You can upgrade the gem by typing:

#raw(block: true, "$ gem install asciidoctor")

When you install a new version of the gem using #raw("gem install"), you end up with multiple versions installed.
Use the following command to remove the old versions:

#raw(block: true, "$ gem cleanup asciidoctor")

= Usage <usage>

If the Asciidoctor gem installed successfully, the #raw("asciidoctor") command line interface (CLI) will be available on your PATH.
To verify it’s available, run the following in your terminal:

#raw(block: true, "$ asciidoctor --version")

You should see information about the Asciidoctor version and your Ruby environment printed in the terminal.

#adoc-role(("output",))[
#raw(block: true, "Asciidoctor 2.0.22 [https://asciidoctor.org]\nRuntime Environment (ruby 3.0.1p64 [x86_64-linux]) (lc:UTF-8 fs:UTF-8 in:UTF-8 ex:UTF-8)")
]

== Command line interface (CLI) <command-line-interface-cli>

The #raw("asciidoctor") command allows you to invoke Asciidoctor from the command line (i.e., a terminal).

The following command converts the file README.adoc to HTML and saves the result to the file README.html in the same directory.
The name of the generated HTML file is derived from the source file by changing its file extension to #raw(".html").

#raw(block: true, "$ asciidoctor README.adoc")

You can control the Asciidoctor processor by adding various flags and switches, which you can learn about using:

#raw(block: true, "$ asciidoctor --help")

For instance, to write the file to a different directory, use:

#raw(block: true, "$ asciidoctor -D output README.adoc")

The #raw("asciidoctor") #link("https://asciidoctor.org/man/asciidoctor")[man page] provides a complete reference of the command line interface.

Refer to the following resources to learn more about how to use the #raw("asciidoctor") command.

- #link("https://docs.asciidoctor.org/asciidoctor/latest/cli/")[Process AsciiDoc using the CLI]
- #link("https://docs.asciidoctor.org/asciidoctor/latest/cli/options/")[CLI options]

== Ruby API <ruby-api>

Asciidoctor also provides an API.
The API is intended for integration with other Ruby software, such as Rails, GitHub, and GitLab, as well as other languages, such as Java (via AsciidoctorJ) and JavaScript (via Asciidoctor.js).

To use Asciidoctor in your application, you first need to require the gem:

#raw(block: true, lang: "ruby", "require 'asciidoctor'")

You can then convert an AsciiDoc source file to an HTML file using:

#raw(block: true, lang: "ruby", "Asciidoctor.convert_file 'README.adoc', to_file: true, safe: :safe")

#adoc-admonition("warning")[
When using Asciidoctor via the API, the default safe mode is #raw(":secure").
In secure mode, several core features are disabled, including the #raw("include") directive.
If you want to enable these features, you’ll need to explicitly set the safe mode to #raw(":server") (recommended) or #raw(":safe").
]

You can also convert an AsciiDoc string to embeddable HTML (for inserting in an HTML page) using:

#raw(block: true, lang: "ruby", "content = '_Zen_ in the art of writing https://asciidoctor.org[AsciiDoc].'\nAsciidoctor.convert content, safe: :safe")

If you want the full HTML document, enable the #raw("header_footer") option as follows:

#raw(block: true, lang: "ruby", "content = '_Zen_ in the art of writing https://asciidoctor.org[AsciiDoc].'\nhtml = Asciidoctor.convert content, header_footer: true, safe: :safe")

If you need access to the parsed document, you can split the conversion into discrete steps:

#raw(block: true, lang: "ruby", "content = '_Zen_ in the art of writing https://asciidoctor.org[AsciiDoc].'\ndocument = Asciidoctor.load content, header_footer: true, safe: :safe\nputs document.doctitle\nhtml = document.convert")

Keep in mind that if you don’t like the output Asciidoctor produces, #emph[you can change it!]
Asciidoctor supports custom converters that can handle converting from the parsed document to the generated output.

One easy way to customize the output piecemeal is by using the template converter.
The template converter allows you to supply a #link("https://github.com/rtomayko/tilt")[Tilt]-supported template file to handle converting any node in the document.

However you go about it, you #emph[can] have 100% control over the output.
For more information about how to use the API or to customize the output, see:

- #link("https://docs.asciidoctor.org/asciidoctor/latest/api/")[Process AsciiDoc using the API]
- #link("https://docs.asciidoctor.org/asciidoctor/latest/api/options/")[API options]
- #link("https://docs.asciidoctor.org/asciidoctor/latest/safe-modes/")[Safe modes]

= Contributing <contributing>

New contributors are always welcome!
If you discover errors or omissions in the source code, documentation, or website content, please don’t hesitate to submit an issue or open a pull request with a fix.

Here are some ways #strong[you] can contribute:

- by using prerelease (alpha, beta or preview) versions
- by reporting bugs
- by suggesting new features
- by writing or editing documentation
- by writing code with tests — #emph[No patch is too small.]
  - fix typos
  - add comments
  - clean up inconsistent whitespace
  - write tests!
- by refactoring code
- by fixing #link("https://github.com/asciidoctor/asciidoctor/issues")[issues]
- by reviewing patches

The #link("CONTRIBUTING.adoc")[Contributing] guide provides information on how to create, style, and submit issues, feature requests, code, and documentation to Asciidoctor.

